pseudo-Boolean proof version 3.0
% Regression test (availability): this proof checks fine, but `--elaborate`
% used to abort the process at
% `elaboration::write_helpers::write_proofgoal_id`
% ("database proofgoal should have same ID as a database constraint",
% SIGABRT, 0-byte output).
%
% `Proofgoal::autoprove` discharges a database goal by finding an equal
% constraint in the database and then writes the subproof with
% `write_existing_constraint_subproof`. It passed the FOUND constraint where the
% header needs the GOAL: a `ProofgoalID::Database(id)` is written as the output
% ID registered for input ID `id`, which only the goal carries (proofgoal
% generation copies the ids of the constraint it substituted), while the found
% constraint is a different object with its own ids. `impl ConstraintLookup for
% Rc<DBConstraint>` ignores the index and hands the found constraint straight
% back, so the lookup silently succeeded and `get_out_id(id)` returned `None`.
%
% Reaching the path needs the db-lookup discharge to be *deferred* out of
% proofgoal generation, which happens while elaborating as long as the touched
% constraints stay under `IGNORE_DATABASE_PROOFGOAL_THRESHOLD` (5%) of the
% database -- hence the 39 padding constraints. Substituting `xv -> 1` into
% constraint 1 yields constraint 2 exactly, which is the goal that gets
% discharged by database lookup.
f 41;
red 1 xv >= 1 : xv -> 1 ;
output NONE;
conclusion NONE;
end pseudo-Boolean proof;
